.TH CVECTOR_RESERVE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_reserve \- reserve capacity for future elements
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

bool cvector_reserve(cvec v, size_t new_capacity_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Reserves capacity for at least new_capacity_count elements, rounding the
capacity up to the nearest power of two (minimum 4). Reserving the
expected size up front saves you a series of reallocations.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v                    Vector to reserve capacity for
new_capacity_count   Minimum number of elements to reserve
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true when the reservation succeeds, and also when the capacity is already
large enough.
false when the allocation fails, when the capacity would be above
ccol_max_elem_count, or when the capacity times the element size
overflows a size_t.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A request <= the current capacity does nothing. Because of the rounding to
a power of 2 the real capacity can be larger than requested.

Both limits of RETURN VALUE are tested against the rounded-up capacity,
not against new_capacity_count itself, so a request within a limit can
still fail after the rounding. A vector of wide elements hits the size_t
overflow limit first. That limit caps the capacity at SIZE_MAX divided by
the element size, which for any element wider than one byte is the lower
of the two.

The element count doesn't change. Asserts if v is NULL.

The cvec_reserve(3) macro evaluates new_capacity_count exactly once, on
the failure path too, so an expression with a side effect is safe to
pass.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_reserve (3)
